{
  "concept": "Because ΔG = ΔH − TΔS, the sign of ΔH fixes where a reaction's free energy starts at low temperature and the sign of ΔS fixes whether ΔG rises or falls with temperature — so only reactions whose ΔH and ΔS share a sign switch between spontaneous and nonspontaneous.",
  "figureType": "line_plot",
  "elements": [
    "Vertical axis labeled 'Free energy' with a single tick value '0'",
    "Horizontal axis labeled 'Increasing temperature (K)' with no numerals",
    "Gray horizontal dashed line at ΔG = 0 spanning the plot width",
    "Pink shaded band filling the entire region above the dashed zero line",
    "Green shaded band filling the entire region below the dashed zero line",
    "Region label 'Nonspontaneous' in the upper right of the pink band",
    "Region label 'Spontaneous' in the lower right of the green band",
    "Axis-side annotation 'ΔG > 0' rotated vertically in the upper pink band near the y-axis",
    "Axis-side annotation 'ΔG < 0' rotated vertically in the lower green band near the y-axis",
    "Red line: starts high in the pink band at the left edge and rises to upper right (endothermic, entropy-decreasing)",
    "Inline rotated label on the red line: 'ΔH > 0, ΔS < 0'",
    "Yellow line A: starts in the pink band at the same left-edge height as the red line and falls, crossing the dashed zero line into green",
    "Inline rotated label on yellow line A: 'ΔH > 0, ΔS > 0'",
    "Yellow line B: starts in the green band at the left edge and rises, crossing the dashed zero line into pink",
    "Inline rotated label on yellow line B: 'ΔH < 0, ΔS < 0'",
    "Green line: starts in the green band at the same left-edge height as yellow line B and falls to the lower right",
    "Inline rotated label on the green line: 'ΔH < 0, ΔS > 0'",
    "Crossing point where the two yellow lines intersect the zero line at different x positions",
    "Movable vertical temperature cursor line with four point markers, one on each line (added by interaction)",
    "Readout panel showing current ΔG sign and spontaneity verdict for each of the four cases",
    "Legend mapping line color to the ΔH/ΔS sign pair"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "temperature",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "Temperature (K)",
      "range": [0, 1000, 10],
      "default": 200,
      "teaches": "That ΔG is read off a line at one temperature, and that as temperature rises the TΔS term grows until it can outweigh ΔH and flip a reaction's spontaneity."
    },
    {
      "id": "caseSelect",
      "type": "toggle",
      "label": "Sign combination",
      "options": ["All four", "ΔH>0, ΔS<0", "ΔH>0, ΔS>0", "ΔH<0, ΔS<0", "ΔH<0, ΔS>0"],
      "default": "All four",
      "teaches": "That the intercept of each line is set by ΔH (which half of the plot it starts in) and its slope is set by −ΔS (whether it rises or falls), so the two signs fully determine the line."
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "The map: two halves, one dashed line",
      "narration": "Above the gray dashed line ΔG is positive and the process is nonspontaneous; below it ΔG is negative and the process is spontaneous. Every line here is just ΔG = ΔH − TΔS plotted against T.",
      "state": { "caseSelect": "All four", "temperature": 0 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Never spontaneous: ΔH > 0, ΔS < 0",
      "narration": "The red line starts in the pink half (positive ΔH) and climbs as temperature rises, because −TΔS is positive. It never reaches zero — heating only makes things worse.",
      "state": { "caseSelect": "ΔH>0, ΔS<0", "temperature": 700 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Always spontaneous: ΔH < 0, ΔS > 0",
      "narration": "The green line starts in the green half (negative ΔH) and falls further as temperature rises. Both terms favor the reaction, so it is spontaneous at every temperature.",
      "state": { "caseSelect": "ΔH<0, ΔS>0", "temperature": 700 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Turns on when heated: ΔH > 0, ΔS > 0",
      "narration": "This yellow line begins nonspontaneous but slopes downward. Drag the temperature cursor past its x-intercept and the marker drops into the green half — the entropy term has overtaken the enthalpy term.",
      "state": { "caseSelect": "ΔH>0, ΔS>0", "temperature": 620 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Turns off when heated: ΔH < 0, ΔS < 0",
      "narration": "The other yellow line starts spontaneous but slopes upward, crossing zero at its own temperature. Above that point the unfavorable entropy change wins and the reaction stops being spontaneous.",
      "state": { "caseSelect": "ΔH<0, ΔS<0", "temperature": 620 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Sweep all four at once",
      "narration": "With all four lines showing, slide the temperature cursor from cold to hot: the red and green markers stay on their own sides, while only the two yellow markers cross the dashed line — each at a different crossover temperature.",
      "state": { "caseSelect": "All four", "temperature": 900 }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "ΔH decides which side of the zero line the reaction starts on and ΔS decides which way it tilts as temperature rises — so spontaneity can only reverse when the two signs agree, and it reverses exactly at the temperature where ΔG = 0.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Portrait-oriented line plot. Y-axis 'Free energy' with the sole tick label '0' roughly at mid-height; X-axis 'Increasing temperature (K)' with no numerals or ticks. A gray dashed horizontal line at y = 0 splits the plot: the entire upper region is filled soft pink, the lower region soft pale green. Large dark region labels 'Nonspontaneous' (upper right) and 'Spontaneous' (lower right). Rotated vertical annotations 'ΔG > 0' and 'ΔG < 0' just inside the left edge, in the upper and lower halves respectively. Four thick straight lines all originating at the left edge: a brick-red line starting high in pink sloping up-right; a mustard/gold line starting at the same left-edge height sloping down through zero into green; a second mustard/gold line starting low in green sloping up through zero into pink; an olive-green line starting at that same low left-edge height sloping down-right. The two yellow lines cross each other near the zero line but reach zero at slightly different x positions. Each line carries a two-line italic label rotated to match its slope, e.g. 'ΔH > 0' over 'ΔS < 0'. Interaction adds a vertical cursor line at the selected temperature with a filled circular marker on each line, markers tinted by which region they currently sit in, plus a small side readout listing each case's ΔG sign. When a single case is selected, the other three lines fade to light gray and the chosen line's left-edge intercept and slope direction are annotated (e.g. 'intercept = ΔH', 'slope = −ΔS')."
}

